-- title:
--   $\pi_1$ of the plane minus $n$ points is free of rank $n$
-- statement:
--   Let $S \subset \mathbb{C}$ be a finite set of $n$ points and let $x$ be a point of $\mathbb{C} \setminus S$. Then the fundamental group of the punctured plane at $x$ is a free group of rank $n$:
--   $$ \pi_1(\mathbb{C}\setminus S,\, x) \;\cong\; F_n . $$
--
--   The punctured plane deformation retracts onto a wedge of $n$ circles, one small loop around each puncture, and the fundamental group of a wedge of $n$ circles is free on those loops. Concretely, free generators may be taken to be the classes of loops $\gamma_1,\dots,\gamma_n$ based at $x$, where $\gamma_i$ encircles the $i$-th puncture once and encircles no other.
--
--   The degenerate case $n = 0$ is included: $\mathbb{C}$ is simply connected and the free group of rank $0$ is trivial.
--
--   This is one of the two inputs to the realization of arbitrary finite groups over $\mathbb{C}(t)$. It is what makes surjections from the fundamental group of a punctured line onto a prescribed finite group available: a finite group generated by $n$ elements receives a surjection from $F_n$, hence from $\pi_1(\mathbb{C}\setminus S, x)$ whenever $S$ has $n$ points. The Riemann existence theorem then converts such a surjection into a Galois extension of $\mathbb{C}(t)$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The punctured plane is presented as the subtype of $\mathbb{C}$ consisting of the points lying outside $S$, carrying its subspace topology, and $n$ is the cardinality of the finite set $S$. The conclusion asserts that the type of group isomorphisms onto the free group on $n$ generators is nonempty; no particular choice of generators is singled out.
-- source:
--   Standard algebraic topology: the plane minus n points is homotopy equivalent to a wedge of n circles, whose fundamental group is free of rank n by the Seifert-van Kampen theorem. See Hatcher, Algebraic Topology, Example 1.21 and Corollary 1.21 (wedge of circles), and Forster, Lectures on Riemann Surfaces, section 4. Used as an input to the realization of finite groups over C(t) in Voelklein, Groups as Galois Groups, ch. 2, and Serre, Topics in Galois Theory, ch. 1

import Mathlib

namespace InverseGalois

theorem fundamentalGroup_punctured_plane_free
    (S : Finset ℂ) (x : ℂ) (hx : x ∉ (S : Set ℂ)) :
    Nonempty (FundamentalGroup {z : ℂ // z ∉ (S : Set ℂ)} ⟨x, hx⟩
      ≃* FreeGroup (Fin S.card)) := by sorry

end InverseGalois
